<%# The one form a set is written through, rendered by both sets/new.erb and sets/edit.erb.
    @set is the row being edited, and nil on the new-set form; that is the whole of the
    difference between the two, and it decides where the form posts and whether the fields
    come up filled in. required rides on it too: a set logged with no weight and no rep
    count records nothing, while whether saving an existing row may empty those fields is a
    separate question neither form has ever asked. The two were separate copies until now
    and both had to be edited in step anyway -- #132 made the same min and step change
    twice over, and #121 the same escaping opt-outs. %>
<%- @action = "/workouts/#{@workout.id}/sets/#{@set ? @set.id : 'new'}" %>
<form action="<%= @action %>" method="post">
  <%== csrf_tag(@action) %>
  <fieldset>
    <div class="space-y-5">
      <%# The movement, which used to be the select this now wraps. #525 is the whole of why
          it moved into a partial of its own: the control is a search box over the same
          options, the select is still underneath it carrying the value, and the argument for
          that arrangement -- including why there is no search endpoint and no htmx on this
          page -- runs longer than the rest of this form put together. %>
      <%== render('sets/_exercise_picker') %>
      <%# step="5" was wrong here in two different ways, one per copy. With no value and no
          min the new-set form counted fives from zero, so it took 135 and 140 and refused
          137 outright; the edit form counted them from the row's own weight, so a set at
          138 could go to 143 but not to 140. step is "any" and min is 0 for the same
          reasons as on the session screen, where the long version of the argument is --
          including why it stopped being 1 when #141 widened the column. %>
      <div>
        <label for="weight" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Weight</label>
        <input type="number" id="weight" name="weight" autocomplete="off" min="0" step="any" inputmode="numeric"
          <%= 'required' unless @set %>
          value="<%= weight_label(@set&.weight) %>"
          class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-full py-1.5 text-gray-900 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
      </div>
      <%# A set counts in reps or in seconds, and the database refuses a row holding both or
          neither. This asked for reps whatever the set was measured in, so a plank came up
          with an empty Reps box, no way to reach the duration it is actually stored in, and
          a rep count typed into it violated the constraint outright -- an unrescued
          exception, which is a 500 and a lost edit. That is #213.

          The set's own measure decides, not the form: a plank is held and a squat is
          repped, which is a property of the movement rather than something an edit to the
          weight may change. A new set has no measure yet and takes reps, which is what the
          column defaults to and what every set this form has ever created was. %>
      <%- if @set&.timed? %>
        <div>
          <label for="duration_seconds" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Seconds</label>
          <input type="number" id="duration_seconds" name="duration_seconds" autocomplete="off" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric"
            value="<%= @set&.duration_seconds %>"
            class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-full py-1.5 text-gray-900 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
        </div>
      <%- else %>
        <div>
          <label for="reps" class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900">Reps</label>
          <input type="number" id="reps" name="reps" autocomplete="off" min="0" step="1" inputmode="numeric"
            <%= 'required' unless @set %>
            value="<%= @set&.reps %>"
            class="<%= field_style %> mt-1 block w-full py-1.5 text-gray-900 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
        </div>
      <%- end %>
      <%# An unticked box sends nothing, and the route reads a missing parameter as false,
          so a box that does not arrive carrying the row's own state clears it on save.
          Both come up clear on the new-set form, where there is no state to carry.

          The three colour classes are #163's, which the new-set copy carried and the edit
          copy, whose boxes wore no classes at all, did not. accent-lime-500 is the one that
          paints the box: text-* only tints a checkbox under @tailwindcss/forms, and
          layout.erb takes the CDN plain, so the lime beside it is doing nothing today. It
          stays because the day that plugin arrives is a strange one to find out this form
          disagrees with every other one -- which is how the Tailwind UI indigo these
          replace lasted as long as it did. Same three classes on views/exercises/_form.erb. %>
      <div class="relative flex items-start">
        <div class="flex h-6 items-center">
          <input id="is_completed" aria-describedby="is_completed-description" name="is_completed" type="checkbox"
            <%= 'checked' if @set && @set[:is_completed] %>
            class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
        </div>
        <div class="ml-3 text-sm leading-6">
          <label for="is_completed" class="font-medium text-gray-900">Completed?</label>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="relative flex items-start">
        <div class="flex h-6 items-center">
          <input id="is_warmup" aria-describedby="is_warmup-description" name="is_warmup" type="checkbox"
            <%= 'checked' if @set && @set[:is_warmup] %>
            class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
        </div>
        <div class="ml-3 text-sm leading-6">
          <label for="is_warmup" class="font-medium text-gray-900">Warmup set?</label>
        </div>
      </div>
      <%# Drawn only where it can mean something, which is the same rule the Reps and
          Seconds boxes above follow. Commands bracket a rep -- start, press, rack -- so a
          movement held for time has no rep for them to bracket, and the database refuses
          the row outright. A box that could only ever produce a 500 is worse than no box.

          The description says what "commanded" means, because it is the one word on this
          form that is not self-evident and because getting it wrong is quiet: a lifter who
          reads it as "paused" ticks it on tempo work, and the count of commanded reps in a
          block -- which is the whole reason the column exists -- quietly stops being true.
          #311. %>
      <%- unless @set&.timed? %>
        <div class="relative flex items-start">
          <div class="flex h-6 items-center">
            <input id="is_commanded" aria-describedby="is_commanded-description" name="is_commanded" type="checkbox"
              <%= 'checked' if @set && @set[:is_commanded] %>
              class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
          </div>
          <div class="ml-3 text-sm leading-6">
            <label for="is_commanded" class="font-medium text-gray-900">Commanded?</label>
            <p id="is_commanded-description" class="text-gray-500">Done under meet commands &mdash; start, press, rack &mdash; rather than at your own tempo.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      <%- end %>
    </div>
    <%# Shape from button_style, which is #173's: one radius, one shadow and one focus ring
        for every primary button in the app. The rounded-lg both copies carried was one of
        the three radii that helper exists to settle. Fill and padding stay here, and
        hover:bg-lime-600 rather than the resting colour repeated is #165's rule -- a
        primary button steps one down its own scale under the pointer, where both copies
        named lime-500 twice and so did not move at all. %>
    <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> mt-2 bg-lime-500 px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">
      Save
    </button>
  </fieldset>
</form>
